<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman law</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman law is the legal system developed in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a> through centuries of statutes, magistrates' edicts and the writings of jurists. It was systematised in the sixth century under the Byzantine emperor Justinian in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> profiles present Roman law as one of their most enduring legacies, forming the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> traditions of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and beyond.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a>, Roman private law was the ius civile, which applied only to Roman citizens, was bound to religion and was strictly formal. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>'s traditional account, the plebeian tribune C. Terentilius Arsa proposed that the law be written down to stop magistrates applying it arbitrarily. Ten men, the decemviri, were chosen in 451 BCE and produced ten tablets in 450 BCE; a second board is said to have added two more in 449 BCE. Modern scholars doubt that a second decemvirate took place and consider it unlikely that an official delegation went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p>The original text was probably destroyed when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 387 BCE. The surviving fragments show that the Tables were not a code in the modern sense but specific provisions altering customary law, mostly on private law and civil procedure. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-hortensia">Lex Hortensia</a> of 287 BCE later made the decisions of plebeian assemblies binding on all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s most important contribution, in this view, was a class of professional jurists and a legal science, rather than well-drafted statutes. Between about 201 and 27 BCE the praetors' edicts created a body of praetoric law that supplemented the formal ius civile and drew on the ius gentium, the law of peoples. Around 130 CE the jurist Salvius Iulianus drafted a standard form of the praetor's edict, which later jurists such as Paulus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulpian">Ulpian</a> commented on.</p><p>Gaius's Institutes divided private law into persons, things and legal actions, a scheme later reflected in Blackstone's Commentaries, the French Code civil and the German BGB. A person's rights depended on status: citizen or foreigner, free or slave, head of a family or a member under another's authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By the middle of the 3rd century jurisprudence had all but ended in the West, and classical law gave way to a vulgar law. In the East, Justinian's Corpus Juris Civilis of 529 to 534 remained the basis of legal practice. In the West the Digest was largely ignored until a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was rediscovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> around 1070. Glossators then studied it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, whose law school gradually became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s first university.</p><p>By the middle of the 16th century Roman law, mixed with canon law and feudal custom, formed the ius commune of continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the Nordic countries did not take part in its wholesale reception. National codes, beginning with the French civil code of 1804 and ending with the German BGB in 1900, brought this practice to an end. The legal systems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-marino">San Marino</a> are still based on the old ius commune, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> system spread through colonial expansion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman law” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of the Roman landowning class was dispossessed or fled, and the Arian Vandal kings persecuted the Catholic clergy, though <b>Roman law</b>, Latin culture and town life continued.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the most systematic codification of <b>Roman law</b> ever undertaken.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its towns adopted Latin, <b>Roman law</b> and urban forms, places such as Segobriga, Bilbilis and Clunia flourished as Roman municipalities under Augustus and his successors, and the Celtiberian language and script disappeared from inscriptions by the first century CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Written law and notarial culture, rooted in <b>Roman law</b>, were strong, as across the Midi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The civil law system rests on <b>Roman law</b>, with the Federal Constitutional Court holding judicial review, specialised supreme courts for labour, social, fiscal and administrative matters, and lay judges, the Schöffen, sitting with professionals on most criminal benches.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Written law codes such as the Pactus Legis Salicae, probably first issued under Clovis, applied to Franks, while Gallo-Romans continued to live under <b>Roman law</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
